##
   Two unrelated types may each declare a method of the same name. The callee is
   chosen at compile time from the receiver's *declared* type, so `l.get()` and
   `r.get()` call `Left_get` and `Right_get` respectively, and neither needs a
   runtime type.

   This is the guard against dynamic dispatch creeping in: there is no vtable,
   because there is nothing to look one up in. If MIR ever grew a receiver
   concept, the two `Call` instructions below would stop being plain
   `FunctionRef` calls.

   The two types deliberately differ in shape. Two *structurally identical*
   struct declarations currently share a single MIR type -- a pre-existing
   interning behaviour, unrelated to methods and reproducible with no `impl` at
   all (see KNOWN_GAPS.md 2.13).
##
struct Left { a: i32 }
struct Right { a: i32, b: i32 }

impl Left {
  func get(self: Left): i32 { return self.a; }
}

impl Right {
  func get(self: Right): i32 { return self.b; }
}

func both(): i32 {
  var l = .Left{.a = 5i32};
  var r = .Right{.a = 1i32, .b = 2i32};
  return l.get() + r.get();
}
